<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_with.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-with">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-with">
  <primary>WITH</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>WITH</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>WITH</refname>
  <refpurpose>project the working rows and pass them to the next clause</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
WITH [ DISTINCT | ALL ] { * | <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable> [ AS <replaceable class="parameter">alias</replaceable> ] } [, ...] [ WHERE <replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable> ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-with-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>WITH</command> closes one section of a query and opens the next. It evaluates its
   expressions once per row exactly as <xref linkend="cypher-return"/> does, then hands the
   resulting columns to the clause that follows. It is a <command>RETURN</command> in the
   middle of a query.
  </para>

  <para>
   Three jobs need it. Aggregating and then filtering or matching further, because an
   aggregate cannot appear in a <xref linkend="cypher-where"/>. Computing a value once and
   using it in several later clauses. Cutting the rows down before the expensive part of the
   query runs.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>WITH</command> replaces the scope. Only what it projects survives. A variable it
   omits is gone, and naming that variable later raises
   <literal>variable does not exist</literal>. Write <literal>WITH *, expr AS v</literal>, or
   use <xref linkend="cypher-let"/>, to add a column without dropping any.
  </para>

  <para>
   Each output name has to be unique within the clause. Projecting the same name twice raises
   <literal>WITH passes on "..." more than once</literal>. <command>RETURN</command> has no
   such rule, because no later clause has to resolve its names.
  </para>

  <para>
   The <literal>WHERE</literal> of a <command>WITH</command> runs after the projection and
   after any grouping, so it can test the alias of an aggregate.
   <command>ORDER BY</command>, <command>SKIP</command>, <literal>OFFSET</literal> and
   <command>LIMIT</command> are clauses in their own right that follow the whole
   <command>WITH</command>, and so follow its <literal>WHERE</literal> too: the filter runs
   first and the paging applies to what survives. See <xref linkend="cypher-orderby"/>,
   <xref linkend="cypher-skip"/> and <xref linkend="cypher-limit"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>WITH</command> can neither open a query nor end one. At the start of a statement
   <literal>WITH</literal> is the SQL common table expression, a different clause taking a
   name and a parenthesized query. <command>LET</command> has no such collision and may open
   a query.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-with-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>DISTINCT</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Removes duplicate rows from the projection before passing them on.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>ALL</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Keeps duplicate rows. This is the default.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>*</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Carries every named binding in scope through unchanged. Combine it with further items,
      as in <literal>WITH *, expr AS v</literal>, to add a column without dropping any.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Any Cypher expression over the bindings in scope, including an aggregate. See
      <xref linkend="cypher-expr"/>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">alias</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The name the next clause uses. It is required for anything that is not a plain variable
      reference, because a later clause has to be able to name it.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A boolean expression filtering the projected rows, evaluated after the projection, so it
      can read an alias defined in this clause.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-with-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Aggregate, then filter on the aggregate. <literal>count(m)</literal> groups by
   <literal>p.name</literal> and the <literal>WHERE</literal> tests the resulting
   <literal>films</literal>. In a <literal>MATCH ... WHERE</literal> the same condition raises
   <literal>aggregate functions are not allowed in WHERE</literal>:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie)
WITH p.name AS actor, count(m) AS films
WHERE films &gt; 1
RETURN actor, films
ORDER BY films DESC, actor;

          actor          | films
-------------------------+-------
 "Keanu Reeves"          | 4
 "Arnold Schwarzenegger" | 3
 "Carrie-Anne Moss"      | 2
 "Harrison Ford"         | 2
 "Laurence Fishburne"    | 2
 "Linda Hamilton"        | 2
(6 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Add a column without losing the others. <literal>WITH *</literal> keeps
   <literal>p</literal> and <literal>m</literal> in scope, so <command>RETURN</command> can
   still read their properties:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[:directed]-&gt;(m:movie)
WITH *, m.year - p.born AS age_at_release
RETURN p.name AS director, m.title AS film, age_at_release
ORDER BY age_at_release
LIMIT 3;

     director      |       film       | age_at_release
-------------------+------------------+----------------
 "James Cameron"   | "The Terminator" | 30
 "Lilly Wachowski" | "The Matrix"     | 32
 "Andrew Niccol"   | "Gattaca"        | 33
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A variable the projection omits is out of scope afterwards:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie)
WITH m
RETURN p.name;

ERROR:  variable does not exist
LINE 3: RETURN p.name;
               ^
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Deduplicate in the middle of a query:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie)
WITH DISTINCT m.genre AS genre
RETURN genre
ORDER BY genre;

    genre
-------------
 "Action"
 "Adventure"
 "Sci-Fi"
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The <literal>WHERE</literal> belongs to the <command>WITH</command>, while
   <command>ORDER BY</command> and <command>LIMIT</command> are the clauses after it, so the
   <command>LIMIT</command> counts only rows that passed the filter:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie)
WITH m.title AS film, m.genre AS genre WHERE genre = 'Action'
ORDER BY film LIMIT 3
RETURN film, genre;

             film             |  genre
------------------------------+----------
 "Terminator 2: Judgment Day" | "Action"
 "The Avengers"               | "Action"
 "The Matrix"                 | "Action"
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Two items of one <command>WITH</command> cannot share an output name. Carrying a variable
   and then aliasing something else to its name collides the same way, so
   <literal>WITH m, m.title AS m</literal> raises the same error:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie)
WITH m.title AS film, m.genre AS film
RETURN film;

ERROR:  WITH passes on "film" more than once
LINE 2: WITH m.title AS film, m.genre AS film
                              ^
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-with-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   The <literal>WHERE</literal> has to be written directly after the projection list, before
   any <command>ORDER BY</command>, <command>SKIP</command> or <command>LIMIT</command>.
   Putting it later is a syntax error:
   <literal>WITH ... ORDER BY film WHERE genre = 'Action'</literal> does not parse.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-with-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 has no <command>WITH</command> clause. It splits the work three ways:
   the return statement of section 14.11 projects and ends a query, the LET statement of
   section 14.7 adds bindings without dropping any, and the next statement of section 9.2
   composes two whole queries. AgensGraph implements all three, as
   <xref linkend="cypher-return"/>, <xref linkend="cypher-let"/> and
   <xref linkend="cypher-next"/>. None of them drops a binding partway through a query, which
   is what <command>WITH</command> is for.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>WITH</command> is the openCypher spelling and behaves as it does there, including
   its <literal>WHERE</literal>, its <literal>DISTINCT</literal> and its
   <literal>*</literal>. openCypher writes <command>ORDER BY</command>,
   <command>SKIP</command> and <command>LIMIT</command> as trailing modifiers of a
   <command>WITH</command>; AgensGraph accepts that and also accepts each of the three as a
   clause of its own. <literal>ALL</literal> is not part of openCypher.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

